WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.250
OK, thank you all for coming today

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.759
to our conference entitled

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.109
"Rokhl Oyerbakh: The Bridge Between

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:16.909
Wartime and Postwar Testimony." My

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.019
name is Stephen Naron and I'm the

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.129
director of the Fortunoff Video

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.379
Archive for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.089
This is the 12th event in the

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.009
Fortunoff Archives 2019 event

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.049
series marking 40 years of

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.699
recording video testimony.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.829
And indeed, about 40 years ago,

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.989
on May 2, 1979,

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:34.759
Laurel Vlock and Dori Laub recorded

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.649
the first four testimonies that would

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.689
establish the Holocaust Survivors Film

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.519
Project, the immediate predecessor to

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.139
the Yale's Fortunoff Video Archive for

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.969
Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.189
And we are still recording testimony

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:47.509
today.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.129
In fact, a few weeks ago, Joanne Rudof

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.719
and Sue Millen recorded a testimony

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.759
with a French child survivor at

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.769
Yale's broadcast studio.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:56.869
From its beginning, the Holocaust

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.249
Survivors Film Project was built,

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.829
funded and staffed by many survivors

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.289
as best embodied by William Rosenberg,

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:04.300
a survivor from Czestochowa,

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.039
who served as the president of the

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.260
project prior to its arrival at Yale.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.189
The first 183 recordings

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:14.419
from the Holocaust Survivors Film

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.459
Project came to Yale in 1981

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.439
with the help of Geoffrey Hartman.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.819
Professor Geoffrey Hartman, who fled

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.609
on a Kindertransport from Frankfurt.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:24.349
His wife, Renée, was one of the first

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.409
four survivors recorded in 1979.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:28.939
And under Geoffrey's leadership and

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.319
Joanne Rudof's stewardship, this

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.089
collection grew to encompass more than

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.309
4,400 testimonies

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:35.839
of survivors, witnesses and

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.119
bystanders. We now have 12,000 hours

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.919
of recorded material, in over a dozen

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.049
languages, recorded in a dozen

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:43.050
different countries.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.049
I'd like to thank a number of

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.939
individuals for making this conference

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.799
possible. First, to the speakers who

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.839
have come far and wide, from as far as

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.849
Israel, Germany and Poland.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.079
A shoynen, shoynen dank.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.609
Thank you for your willingness to

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:56.779
share your scholarship, your

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.699
intellectual labor, your dedication

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.139
to what Oyerbakh accomplished and

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.699
stands for.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.619
My thanks to the Fortunoff Blaustein,

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.719
Ross, Libby, and Kohl families

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.129
and the many other donors to the

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.418
archive for their unflagging support.

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:10.489
I'd like to thank our honorary

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.289
advisory board and our Faculty

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.119
Advisors Council in particular.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.099
I'd like to thank Sam Moyn, who

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.509
gave me the creative spark for

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.099
organizing this event series,

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.769
it's a year long event series, and who

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:23.899
helped me, together with Sam Kassow,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.879
build an exciting series with

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:26.989
this conference, this Oyerbakh

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.389
conference at its heart.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:31.459
Although there are several more events

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.409
in the series, and I encourage you all

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.359
to attend, again, I want to emphasize

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.309
this really was the beginning

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.289
of the entire event series and

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.359
marks a sort of climax of this

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.649
year long effort.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.469
So thank you, Sams plural.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.389
I'd like to express my gratitude

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.249
for the support displayed by Susan

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.499
Gibbons, university librarian,

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.209
and my supervisor, Christine Weideman,

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:54.259
the director of Manuscripts and

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.239
Archives, as well as Maurice Samuels

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.399
and Renee Reed at Yale Judaic Studies

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.389
Program.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.339
I'd also like to thank Yale's

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.169
Program for the Study of Antisemitism,

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:04.969
David Simon at Yale's Genocide Studies

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.979
Program, and Doug Rogers at Yale's

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.989
Russian, East European, and Eurasian

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:10.699
Studies Program.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.349
Finally, I'd like to thank the

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:13.759
Fortunoff Archive's staff and

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.069
postdocs, in particular Debra Bush.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.659
Today would not have been possible

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.369
without Debra's incredible

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.349
organizational skill,

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.479
her patience and in

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:24.799
getting every detail right regarding

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.439
travel, logistics and catering, etc.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.089
Debra has worked at Yale for more than

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.249
40 years, and

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:32.809
more than two decades at the Fortunoff

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.099
Video Archive.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.839
She's our institutional memory and

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.090
administrative oracle of Delphi,

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.969
and the heart of the archives,

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.889
sustaining all aspects of the work

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:43.879
we do.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.919
So please join me in thanking her.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.389
I'm certain that I forgot, I forgot

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.679
many other individuals whom I owe

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.599
thanks and I hope they will forgive

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.729
me for my omissions.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.199
Before I introduce today's keynote

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.629
speaker and I'm going to cut my my

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:05.679
presentation short because we're

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:06.680
already running behind,

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:09.459
I just wanted to say a few things

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.439
about why the Fortunoff

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:12.909
Archive is organizing a conference

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.379
like this today.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:15.999
I believe that the connection between

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.169
Oyerbakh's work and the archive is

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:19.148
self-evident, but that connection

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:21.069
may not be as clear to

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.419
the rest of you.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.849
After all, Oyerbakh died in 1976,

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.049
three years before the Fortunoff

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.549
Archive's predecessor organization

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:28.799
began taping in New Haven.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:32.019
But as her-- as head of Yad Vashem's

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.969
oral testimony section, her work

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:35.679
set a path that we would follow and

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.299
expand upon.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.069
Orit Noiman at Yad Vashem was kind

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.749
enough to give me a copy of Oyerbakh's

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.489
Oral Testimony Guidelines, which is an

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.079
18 page Hebrew document which I had

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.009
translated.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.499
It was drafted in 1962.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.569
She compiled it as a manual for

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.339
the poorly paid staff and you can see

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.439
how poorly paid the staff was in the

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.449
guidelines that she's telling them not

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.399
to, you know, not to give up hope

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.469
despite how, how poorly they're

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.449
paid, and the teams

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:02.619
of vol-- a team of volunteers

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.199
recording testimonies for Yad Vashem.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:06.819
When I read it, it was no surprise to

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.739
me that her motivations for

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:09.939
recording testimony and her

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:12.399
methodology so clearly foreshadowed

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.749
the work the Fortunoff Archive would

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.189
start in New Haven.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.529
And I quote, "The testimonies

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.209
we collect from Holocaust survivors

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:20.859
are one of the most important Jewish

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.419
sources to preserve and research a

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.219
time of devastation.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.779
These testimonies will help countless

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.549
future researchers, teachers,

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.769
psychologists, sociologists,

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.629
students and ordinary people learn

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:32.799
about the Holocaust from those who

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:34.929
survived it." She continues,

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.029
making explicit the connection

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:38.949
between past efforts to do this and

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.959
her present work by naming

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.879
Emanuel Ringelblum as the first

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.949
man, the first man

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:46.719
to perform an organized documentation

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.129
work as a way to record the events of

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.049
World War II, already

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:51.969
composing the first questionnaires to

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:53.859
collect, preserve and document the

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:54.860
period.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:56.949
She then turns her attention to

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.049
methodology and purpose, and I can't

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.089
help but smile when I read her words

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:02.619
as they echo the Fortunoff Archive's

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.719
training manual compiled by Joanne

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.999
Rudof and Dana Kline, perhaps

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.529
two of our, our most

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:10.899
Oyerbakh-ian like figures of the

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.219
Fortunoff video archive.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:14.229
And I'll quote again from

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:15.579
Oyerbakh's manual.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.229
"We must allow the witness to speak

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:19.149
freely and we must follow the thread

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:20.739
as naturally as possible.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:22.509
Otherwise we risk losing some of the

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:24.219
trust we built.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:25.839
We must not forget that this testimony

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.909
is in fact an autobiography and

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:29.889
it's first and foremost the witnesses

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:30.890
story."

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:34.579
And so with that,

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:37.110
that's an end quote.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.859
And now I, it's my pleasure to

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.809
introduce our keynote speaker, Sam

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:42.810
Kassow.

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:44.929
Sam is the Charles Northam Professor

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:46.849
of History at Trinity College.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:49.129
He's the author of several works,

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:51.079
monographs and innumerable

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.059
articles. His most recent book

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.709
was the edited volume called "Listen

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:56.299
and Believe: the Ghetto Reportage of

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:58.369
Peretz Opoczynski and Jozeph

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.289
Zelkowicz" from Yale University

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.359
Press in 2015 and

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:03.619
his critically acclaimed work, "Who

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:04.939
Will Write Our History?

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.439
Emanuel Ringelblum and the Oyneg

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:07.759
Shabes Archive" from Indiana

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:08.959
University Press.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.639
That book has been translated into

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:11.640
eight languages?

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:14.329
Eight languages.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.459
It received the 2007 Orbis

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.649
Prize and was a finalist for the 2008

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:20.689
National Jewish Book Award.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.519
Sam has been a visiting scholar at

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.919
Princeton, at JTS, Wesleyan,

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:26.869
many, many others,

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:27.919
and has received a list of

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:29.389
scholarships and fellowships, far too

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:30.390
numerous to name.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.109
Beyond his scholarship, he has made

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.059
numerous significant contributions to

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.069
the field in other capacities.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.019
In particular, one highlight would be

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:40.999
his work developing a section of

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.949
the modern exhibit at

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:45.109
Poland Museum in Warsaw

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.479
and his contribution

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.609
to Roberta Grossman's cinematic

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:51.439
translation of his own book "Who Will

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:52.579
Write Our History?" which, if you have

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.859
not seen that film, it is fantastic

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:56.569
and I believe will be screened at the

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:58.609
YIVO gala in a few weeks.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.109
He has also served appointment, in

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.429
appointments, official appointments

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:06.289
for the Association of Jewish Studies

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:07.519
and is on the board of the Center for

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.439
Jewish History and many, many

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.209
other contributions.

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:12.949
Finally, Sam has always been an

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:14.389
incredible supporter of the Fortunoff

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:16.309
Video Archive and this support,

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:18.469
I think, is both intellectual and

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:19.699
personal.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:21.409
He's a member of our honorary advisory

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:23.419
board, a lifetime appointment honoring

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:25.069
his enormous scholarly achievements,

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:28.099
but also his deep personal connection.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:30.439
After all, he is a New Havener Yid,

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.329
and he spent much of his childhood

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:34.399
among the survivors in New Haven, and

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:35.629
his mother gave testimony to the

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:37.548
Fortunoff Archive twice, once

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:39.889
in 1980 and once in '87.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:41.209
In fact, her testimony will be

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:42.859
featured as an episode in the

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.749
Fortunoff Archive's new podcast

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.579
series, "Those Who Were There," to

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.739
which Sam is also contributing

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:50.569
by composing a series of essays for

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.399
each episode that provide historical

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:53.669
background and context.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:56.419
Sam, the Archive is grateful for your

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:58.759
guidance and support, the generosity

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:00.499
with which you've advised me since

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:02.299
I've returned to Yale to take this

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:04.249
position and your assistance

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:06.379
with arranging today's conference.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:07.820
Please join me in welcoming Sam.

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:19.720
Thanks a lot, and

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:23.859
it's a, it's a great honor

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.049
to speak

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:27.249
at this conference

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:29.840
in,

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.759
on the legacy of Rachel

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:36.760
Oyerbakh,

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.029
a woman who,

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:44.339
while she lived, got much

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.259
less recognition and

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.209
praise than she deserved.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.109
And, of course, many thanks to the

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:53.479
Fortunoff Video Archive,

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.549
to, to Stephen Naron, Debra

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:57.499
Bush, to Yale University, to

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.809
Professor Samuel Moyn

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.960
for making this conference possible.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:05.539
Today, we will hear from many scholars

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:07.879
who, representing different

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:09.829
disciplines, have all

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:12.619
done much to help us

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.539
better understand

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.699
her rich and multifaceted

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:18.049
legacy.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:20.029
Her life was linked

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.519
to such diverse topics

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:25.489
as women in Jewish culture,

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.589
regionalism and folklore.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:29.989
Her key role in

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:31.411
the Galician journal "Tsushtayer,"

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.059
and the Landkentenisher movement.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.729
Her life was linked to pre-war Warsaw

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:40.099
as a Yiddish cultural center.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.589
Of course, the Oyneg Shabes archive

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:44.659
in the Warsaw Ghetto,

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:46.489
the Central Jewish Historical

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:48.769
Commission in postwar

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:50.809
Poland, her involvement

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:52.999
in films such as "Unsere Kinder,"

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.039
which we saw last night, her

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:57.049
very fraught career at Yad

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:59.089
Vashem, the Eichmann

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:01.339
trial where she did yeoman

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.319
work behind the scenes to

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:05.299
bring survivor testimony into the

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:06.300
courtroom.

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:09.319
What Annette Wieviorka called

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.749
"the era of the witness"

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.119
came about in no small

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:16.219
measure, thanks to the

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.259
unstinting efforts of people

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:20.599
like Rokhl Oyerbakh,

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:22.699
their efforts to highlight

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:25.279
the importance of survivor testimony,

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:26.569
the Eichmann trial being a key

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:28.069
example.

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:29.929
At a time when scholars like Raul

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:31.849
Hilberg argued that documents were

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.519
better sources than survivor

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:36.769
accounts, Oyerbakh countered,

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.199
yes, documents are well and good,

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.869
but they were a source that usually

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:44.929
had more to say about perpetrators

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:46.639
than about victims.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:49.189
And long before the work of,

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:51.649
long before the recent synthetic

00:11:51.650 --> 00:11:52.750
history of Saul Friedländer

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:56.659
or the work of Jan Tomasz Gross,

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:58.759
Oyerbakh insisted

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:01.069
that the voice of the victims be heard

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:03.379
in a way that scholars

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:05.599
and jurists take survivor

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:07.999
testimony seriously,

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.639
focusing not just on content

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.919
but also on the nuances of body

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:14.929
language, phrasing,

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.879
affect, language

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:18.049
itself.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:20.329
And so what if those

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:22.489
giving testimony got some

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.739
dates and names wrong?

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:27.019
Their authority of witness

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.389
far outweighed mistakes

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:30.460
in the details.

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:34.379
Oyerbakh began listening to the

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:36.449
victims as early as

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.429
1941 in

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.709
a soup kitchen in the Warsaw ghetto.

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:43.409
As early as 1942,

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:45.569
as she wrote down the testimony

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:47.609
of Avrom Krzepicki, an

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:49.739
escapee from Treblinka.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.659
She did this for the Oyneg Shabes

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:53.999
archive, an encounter that changed

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.159
her life as one of only

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.795
three survivors of the Oyneg Shabes

00:12:57.796 --> 00:13:00.089
arcive, Rokhl

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:02.399
Oyerbakh had the opportunity

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:04.769
not only to retrieve her wartime

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.869
writings, but to rewrite

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:09.209
and publish them

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:11.369
in such books as "B'hutsot

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:13.859
varshe," "Varshever tsavoes,"

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:14.860
and "Baym letsten veg." And

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.809
there were significant differences

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:21.179
between what she wrote during the war

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:23.429
and how she rewrote those same pages

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:24.749
later.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.789
As she handed over her first

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.099
cache of ghetto writings

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.139
to the Oyneg Shabes on

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:34.439
July 26, 1942,

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:36.719
in the middle of the chaos

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.639
and horror of the deportations

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:40.019
to Treblinka.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.029
She attached a note that

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.159
exposed her feelings.

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.559
Raw fear, violent

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.599
anger, despair.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.609
And now I'm quoting: "I'm

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.589
handing over this unfinished essay

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:54.539
to the archive, the fifth

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:55.540
day of the Aktion.

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:58.619
Perhaps such horrors have already

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:00.599
occurred in Jewish history,

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.429
but such shame never.

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:04.769
Jews as tools of the killers.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.179
I want to stay alive.

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.099
I'm ready to kiss the boots

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:10.109
of the worst scoundrel

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.059
just to be able to see the moment

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:13.979
of revenge, revenge,

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:15.059
revenge.

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.060
Remember."

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:18.479
In that same note, she wondered

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:20.099
whether her writings would share the

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.589
same fate as the scribblings

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:24.599
of a coal miner trapped in an

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:26.969
accident with no survivors

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:30.089
whose body would never be found.

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:31.799
Would anyone read those notes or

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:32.699
anyone care?

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:34.439
And besides, she could no longer write

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:35.699
coherently.

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:37.799
And if she survived, what good

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:39.959
would she be to anybody?

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:42.059
Fast forward two years

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:44.639
to early 1944.

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:46.649
She was now on the Aryan side with

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:48.299
false papers.

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:50.459
She no longer had any doubts.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:52.139
She had to write.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:54.179
This was a debt she owed to the dead.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:55.859
But at the same time, she had to be

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:57.179
careful.

00:14:57.180 --> 00:14:58.679
The mass murder, the murder of

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.869
millions of Jews by the Germans

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.449
is a fact that speaks for itself.

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:05.349
It's very dangerous to add to this

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:07.559
subject interpretations

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:08.999
or analyses.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:10.979
Anything that is said can quickly

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:12.959
turn into hopeless hysteria

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:14.999
or endless sobs.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.159
So one must approach the subject

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:18.989
with the greatest caution in a

00:15:18.990 --> 00:15:21.629
restrained and factual manner.

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:23.609
This has been my intention.

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.649
Not to express, but

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.569
to transmit to

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:29.699
note only facts but not

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:32.009
to interpret.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.109
But she quickly added that she

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:36.059
realized immediately

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:38.399
that this mandate was impossible

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:40.559
even for herself.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:43.019
After the war in Poland

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:44.369
and in Israel.

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:46.559
After she'd recovered her wartime

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.689
manuscripts, her writing would take

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.819
on yet another purpose to

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:52.859
ensure that the Jewish people

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:54.089
remember.

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:56.189
Not only to keep faith with

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:58.319
the dead, but also to use

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.329
the lessons of the Holocaust

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:02.399
to strengthen the nation and

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.169
tighten the bonds between Israel and

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:05.759
the diaspora.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:08.099
Oyerbakh left Poland for Israel

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.169
in 1950, and

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.209
while she had many difficulties in

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:14.669
her new home, she never wavered

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:16.889
in her conviction that after

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:19.169
the Holocaust, Israel had become

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:21.089
the vital center of the Jewish

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:22.079
people.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.179
And for those who read Yiddish,

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.129
I highly recommend her book "In

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:28.109
Land Yisroel," a book

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:31.139
of reportage from the early 1950s

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:33.149
that revealed the devotion to the new

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:35.129
state, as well as her

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:36.989
empathy for the struggles of the new

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.029
immigrants, the Jews from the Middle

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:41.039
East and North Africa,

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:43.019
no less than survivors of the

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.779
Holocaust, although her comparisons

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:47.729
of certain Yemenite Jews to Jewish

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:49.889
villagers in rural Galicia

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:51.419
might be something of a stretch.

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:56.689
Like all good reportage,

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:59.209
Oyerbakh's postwar books

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:01.339
conveyed what she saw

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:03.919
and observed in evocative,

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:05.929
powerful language that

00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:07.879
made her writings an indispensable

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:10.039
source for the cultural

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:11.959
and social history of

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.000
the Warsaw ghetto.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:15.060
For Oyerbakh,

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:18.139
memory and mourning demanded

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:20.419
a painful return

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:22.459
to the intense, vibrant world

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:24.588
of pre-war Jewish Warsaw,

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:26.509
which one of her mentors,

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:28.879
the Yiddish poet Melekh Ravitch,

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:30.919
had called a sprawling mosaic

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:32.959
of different Jewish worlds,

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.029
different tribes and subcultures.

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:36.619
Chasidim Polish-speaking

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:37.620
professionals, Litvaks.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:42.139
Oyerbakh took the shattered

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:44.059
bits and shards of that world

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:46.759
and reconstructed a mosaic of memory

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:49.459
based on a poignant evocation

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:52.189
of dozens of individual vignettes.

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:54.259
And thus she spoke for those who

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:56.299
have no one to remember them,

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:58.219
the ordinary Jews she came to

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:00.259
know in the ghetto as she ran

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:02.749
the soup kitchen on Leszno 40

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:04.729
and the Jewish writers, artists and

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:06.709
actors whose milieu

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:08.959
she shared in the 1930s

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:09.960
and in the war.

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:13.549
The more one knew about what

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:16.069
and whom the Germans had murdered,

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:18.559
the more one could grasp the enormity

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:20.359
of the national horror, the national

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:22.009
catastrophe.

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:24.049
As Oyerbakh struggled to describe

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:25.450
the destruction of Warsaw Jewry,

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:28.819
her story elided into a

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:29.942
plaidoyer for Warsaw's particular

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:34.069
place in the history

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:36.409
of the Polish Jews.

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:39.019
In 1945, Oyerbakh

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:41.029
recalled that she

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:42.979
had first come to Warsaw exactly

00:18:42.980 --> 00:18:44.989
20 years before,

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:46.699
during the intermediate days of

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:49.939
Passover 1925,

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:52.039
just as tens of thousands

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:54.319
of Jews were streaming to the Gensha

00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:56.809
cemetery to dedicate

00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:58.789
the Peretz mausoleum, the Oyhel

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:00.859
Perets, which miraculously

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:02.359
survived the war.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.429
And now I'm reading Anita Norich's

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:06.049
translation.

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:07.879
"But that was the first time I'd seen

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:08.719
such a thing.

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:11.359
And I was astonished and deeply moved.

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:14.509
Organized and spontaneous delegations

00:19:14.510 --> 00:19:16.549
swept through the masses of Jews

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:18.589
of all walks of life, wearing

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:20.569
caps and hats and yarmulkes,

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:22.939
businessmen and workers, young

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:24.859
and old, a mobile force

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:25.879
of people.

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:27.769
To my eyes that look like something

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:28.969
out of Asia.

00:19:28.970 --> 00:19:31.069
I could never have seen such a thing

00:19:31.070 --> 00:19:33.709
among Jews in Lwow.

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:35.719
This was Jewish Warsaw.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:37.609
And this was how it expressed its

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:40.039
feelings for one of its spiritual

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:42.169
leaders, as many Jews

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:44.489
as were then gathered in the Gensha

00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:46.669
cemetery are not now

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:48.770
to be found in all of Poland.

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:53.269
Around that same time after the war,

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:55.159
the writer Leo Finkelsteiner, a friend

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:56.391
of Oyerbakh's, wrote in the dos

00:19:56.392 --> 00:19:58.429
amolike Yidishe Varshe

00:19:58.430 --> 00:19:59.779
that Warsaw's strength

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:03.449
derived not from

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:05.479
zkhus oves, but from

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:08.089
zkhus otzma, not from its historical

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:10.159
tradition but from a sheer mass

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:11.509
in power.

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:14.449
Warsaw had no encrusted traditions

00:20:14.450 --> 00:20:16.699
and thus could serve as a path breaker

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:18.690
in the modernization of Jewish life.

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:23.059
What Finkelstein thought of Warsaw

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:24.769
when Finkelstein thought of Warsaw,

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:26.779
what he emphasized like Oyerbakh,

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:28.879
were the crowds, the masses

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:31.399
of Jews who came to Peretz's funeral.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:33.559
To the May Day demonstrations to

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:35.199
see the Hasidic rabbis.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:39.019
By the same token, in a postwar

00:20:39.020 --> 00:20:41.599
essay comparing Vilna and Warsaw,

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:43.759
the literary critic Shlomo Beilis

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.249
wrote, Is it not symbolic

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:47.659
Warsaw struggled?

00:20:47.660 --> 00:20:49.789
He refers to the ghetto uprising.

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:51.799
Wilna furnished the song, the

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:53.749
partizan song, although

00:20:53.750 --> 00:20:55.669
at a critical moment, the Wilna jews

00:20:55.670 --> 00:20:57.430
rejected armed rebellion.

00:20:59.030 --> 00:21:00.949
Is this not a reflection of

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:02.899
the entire history of these two great

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:04.279
Jewish centers?

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:06.319
Warsaw earned its place

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:08.599
in East European Jewish history

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:10.849
because of its quantity,

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:13.039
as well as its quality.

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:14.959
Because of its enormous Jewish

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:16.909
population, she took first

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:18.609
place in the Jewish world.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:22.309
Abraham Lewin, a key member

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:24.109
of the Oyneg Shabes, wrote in late

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:26.149
1942 that

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:28.279
Warsaw was the nerve center of

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:30.559
Polish jewery and Polish

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:32.839
jewery was the cultural center

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:33.950
of the Jewish people.

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:38.019
So how could the Jewish people

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:40.269
survive this massacre

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:42.729
that Lewin, who would soon be murdered

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:44.889
probably in Treblinka, could

00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:47.079
ask this question, only

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:49.869
underscores the special agony

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:51.819
felt by Holocaust victims

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:54.279
who, before they died,

00:21:54.280 --> 00:21:56.229
had to witness the destruction

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:58.179
of their families, the

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:00.039
destruction of their people, of their

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:02.199
language without any

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:04.389
trace of consolation,

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:06.909
such as the knowledge, for instance,

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:08.709
that a Jewish state would arise after

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:09.969
the war.

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:12.579
If Oyerbakh could do nothing else,

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:14.739
she could at least ensure

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:16.659
that posterity remembered

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:18.639
what this great Jewish metropolis

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:19.640
had meant.

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:22.539
Before the war, Oyerbakh

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:24.759
herself admitted to a certain

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:26.359
ambivalence about Jewish Warsaw.

00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:28.659
On the one hand, she fiercely

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:30.879
defended the Jewish masses

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:33.009
against articles

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:35.439
such as a demeaning reportage

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:37.389
Charlie Long, The

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:39.339
Dark Continent, which appeared in

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:41.979
an elite literary journal, Wiadomosci

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:44.529
Literackie, which compared

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:46.539
the Jews of Warsaw to

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:48.729
the denizens of

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:50.769
Africa and saw

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:53.199
them as interesting specimens

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:55.419
worth of anthropological study

00:22:55.420 --> 00:22:56.889
but little more.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:59.049
Oyerbakh went ballistic, and

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:01.779
she accused the author Wanda Melcer

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:03.729
and by extension, the entire

00:23:03.730 --> 00:23:04.730
Wiadomosci Literackie

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.949
milieu with its heavy representation

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:10.719
of brilliant intellectuals of Jewish

00:23:10.720 --> 00:23:13.419
origin, of pretentious arrogance

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:15.369
and ignorance.

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:17.619
Yet on the other hand,

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:20.259
Oyerbakh freely admitted

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:22.659
that before the war, she herself

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:24.339
had shared some of the widespread

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:26.469
pessimism about the future

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:28.509
of Warsaw as a Jewish and

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:30.519
more specifically, a Yiddish cultural

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:31.539
center.

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:33.429
She expressed real doubts about the

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:35.349
cultural future of Yiddish,

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:37.269
especially in light of rampant

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:39.459
acculturation and the economic

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:40.460
woes of Polish Jewry.

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:44.019
But after the war, she revised

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:46.089
her previous skepticism.

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:48.609
It would be easy to dismiss

00:23:48.610 --> 00:23:50.289
Oyerbakh's change of heart

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:53.679
as a post-Holocaust need

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:56.139
to sanctify what had been destroyed.

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:57.630
But that would not be accurate.

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:01.443
Like her mentor Ringelblum,

00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:04.749
writing in late 1943

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:07.359
and '44, Oyerbakh

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:09.399
devoted a large part of her

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:12.219
wartime and postwar writings

00:24:12.220 --> 00:24:14.229
to the Warsaw Jewish

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:16.119
intelligentsia.

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:18.549
Writers like Kalman Lis

00:24:18.550 --> 00:24:21.339
and Joseph Kirman, Yisorael Shtern.

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:24.069
Actresses like Miriam Orleska.

00:24:24.070 --> 00:24:25.909
Painters and sculptors like Avrom

00:24:25.910 --> 00:24:28.809
Ostrzego and Roman Kramsztyk.

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:30.759
Composers and choir

00:24:30.760 --> 00:24:33.729
directors such as David Eisenstadt.

00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:35.649
She described Shie Broyde,

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:36.650
her boss in the Aleynhilf,

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:40.089
who had translated Plato's dialogs

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:42.219
into Yiddish and who, had

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:44.619
he only survived, would have enriched

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:47.409
the Yiddish language beyond compare.

00:24:47.410 --> 00:24:49.419
If only this play

00:24:49.420 --> 00:24:51.609
had survived, if only

00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:53.529
that cantata, if only

00:24:53.530 --> 00:24:55.029
that poem.

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:57.039
What she heard in the ghetto, she

00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:59.559
wrote, represented a flowering

00:24:59.560 --> 00:25:01.509
of Jewish genius, a

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:03.639
bright flame just before the final

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:05.109
destruction.

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:07.149
In her postwar writings,

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:09.549
Oyerbakh openly explained

00:25:09.550 --> 00:25:11.769
that she had doubted the prospects

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:13.869
for Yiddish culture in Poland

00:25:13.870 --> 00:25:15.789
because she had underestimated

00:25:15.790 --> 00:25:18.159
the vitality of the Polish-Jewish

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:19.239
masses.

00:25:19.240 --> 00:25:21.219
It was her work in the Aleynhilf, in

00:25:21.220 --> 00:25:23.259
the self-help, in the soup kitchens

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:27.159
when she came into close contact,

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:29.529
not just with ordinary Jews,

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:31.449
but with the impressive leaders of

00:25:31.450 --> 00:25:32.799
the religious world

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:35.589
that she never would have come to know

00:25:35.590 --> 00:25:36.969
had it not been for the war.

00:25:36.970 --> 00:25:38.949
It was then she began

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:40.779
to understand, according to her

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:42.729
writings, that Jewish

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:45.099
life was so deeply rooted,

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:48.099
the Jews were so numerous

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:49.959
that the nation could have counted on

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:51.909
the capital, the cultural capital

00:25:51.910 --> 00:25:53.799
of Polish Jewry, for generations to

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:54.800
come.

00:25:55.750 --> 00:25:56.750
And now, I quote,

00:25:57.880 --> 00:25:59.949
"Despite everything,

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:02.229
we were surrounded with enormously

00:26:02.230 --> 00:26:04.299
powerful and dynamic Jewish folk

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:06.369
masses, too

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:08.319
numerous and too rooted

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:10.839
in their culture for acculturation

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:12.519
to threaten the basic character of the

00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:13.569
nation.

00:26:13.570 --> 00:26:15.549
The religious strata were an

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:17.529
enormous reservoir of human

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:19.509
resources and spiritual

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:21.519
energy, and they would have made

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:23.829
up any losses that we were suffering

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:27.099
towards Polish culture.

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:29.229
We thought that we had arrived in

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:31.839
Jewish Warsaw too late.

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:33.939
We thought we'd arrived after the best

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:35.629
was already over.

00:26:35.630 --> 00:26:36.630
In fact, the blooms faded

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:39.829
only to prepare for the next harvest

00:26:39.830 --> 00:26:41.899
of fruit, and

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:43.249
this would have happened had it not

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:45.229
been for the terrible catastrophe that

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:46.230
befell us."

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:49.849
As soon as the war ended,

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:51.799
Oyerbakh resolve to carry

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:53.929
on Ringelblum's legacy

00:26:53.930 --> 00:26:55.939
and to find the tin boxes and

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:58.489
milk cans buried under the trackless

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:00.139
rubble of what had been the Warsaw

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:01.140
ghetto.

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:03.937
The Yiddish writer Mendel Mann

00:27:03.938 --> 00:27:05.969
recalled Oyerbakh's talk,

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:08.179
the third anniversary commemoration

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:10.009
of the ghetto uprising.

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:12.709
After many tiresome speeches

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:14.779
full of political posturing,

00:27:14.780 --> 00:27:16.519
Oyerbakh, the only woman on the

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:18.649
podium, got up to speak.

00:27:18.650 --> 00:27:20.359
She didn't make any speeches.

00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:22.309
She didn't explain the meaning of the

00:27:22.310 --> 00:27:24.259
uprising with a stubbornness

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:26.419
that deeply affected me, she demanded.

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:28.729
She called. Remember, she cried out.

00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:30.709
There is a national treasure under

00:27:30.710 --> 00:27:31.669
the ruins.

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:33.529
Even if there are five stories of

00:27:33.530 --> 00:27:35.419
ruins, we have to find the archive.

00:27:35.420 --> 00:27:36.589
I'm not making this up.

00:27:36.590 --> 00:27:37.939
I know what I'm talking about.

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:39.919
This isn't just talk.

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:42.049
This is coming from my heart.

00:27:42.050 --> 00:27:44.119
I will not rest and I will not let you

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:45.874
rest. We must rescue the Ringelblum

00:27:45.875 --> 00:27:46.875
archive.

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:49.219
Now, many people were skeptical.

00:27:49.220 --> 00:27:50.809
They had their own troubles.

00:27:50.810 --> 00:27:52.369
They didn't need the archive to tell

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:54.019
them what they'd been through.

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:56.119
But Oyerbakh disagreed.

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:58.429
Now, more than ever, survivors

00:27:58.430 --> 00:27:59.839
had to organize.

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:01.789
A systematic and collective

00:28:01.790 --> 00:28:03.889
effort to record the past and

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:05.639
to continue the work of the Oyneg

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:06.529
Shabes.

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:08.189
The traditions of the Ringelblum

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:10.669
archive could counteract disturbing

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:13.159
trends that she had already

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:15.259
noticed in early postwar

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:17.269
memoirs. For example,

00:28:17.270 --> 00:28:18.709
the eminent, the memoirs of the

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:21.289
eminent biologist Ludwig Hirschfeld,

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:23.449
a convert to Catholicism,

00:28:23.450 --> 00:28:25.519
or the pianist Wladyslaw

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:27.589
Szpilman, according

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:30.829
to her, presented a skewed

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:33.649
and distorted picture of the ghetto.

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:36.109
But what could one expect, she asked,

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:37.939
from individuals who had been so

00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:39.889
distant from Jewish society before

00:28:39.890 --> 00:28:40.969
the war.

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:42.859
On the other hand, Oyerbakh also

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:45.109
attacked the memoirs and writings

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:47.089
of many nationally conscious

00:28:47.090 --> 00:28:49.639
Jewish survivors for their tendency

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:51.559
to settle political scores

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:54.709
and nurture ideological jealousies.

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:57.259
And this was always a pet peeve

00:28:57.260 --> 00:28:59.179
Oyerbakh's. And again, she held up the

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:01.189
model of the Oyneg Shabes,

00:29:01.190 --> 00:29:03.109
where she believed a shared

00:29:03.110 --> 00:29:05.149
sense of national mission had

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:07.159
trumped political differences.

00:29:07.160 --> 00:29:09.499
In the aftermath of a catastrophe,

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.599
a wounded nation had to look at its

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:13.969
record, the good as well as the bad

00:29:13.970 --> 00:29:16.099
to tell the whole truth, to add

00:29:16.100 --> 00:29:17.719
and subtract nothing.

00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:19.729
This was a debt owed not just to the

00:29:19.730 --> 00:29:21.649
victims who had died,

00:29:21.650 --> 00:29:23.869
but to the nation that had to recover

00:29:23.870 --> 00:29:25.339
and rebuild.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:27.529
Oyerbakh hallowed the exploits

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:29.179
of the ghetto fighters and the

00:29:29.180 --> 00:29:30.409
partisans.

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:32.479
But she did not want their story to

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:35.239
overshadow and diminish

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:37.669
the memory of the 99 percent

00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:39.859
who had no weapons.

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:42.439
Like much of the Oyneg Shabes staff,

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:44.419
Oyerbakh was close to the circles

00:29:44.420 --> 00:29:46.339
of the pre-war YIVO, the

00:29:46.340 --> 00:29:48.289
Yiddish scientific institute, with

00:29:48.290 --> 00:29:50.029
its traditions of breaking down

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:52.369
barriers between scholars and ordinary

00:29:52.370 --> 00:29:54.469
Jews, of zamling,

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:56.659
a mass movement to collect documents

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:58.669
and source materials.

00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:00.589
A key goal of the YIVO

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:02.574
was to explore the everyday,

00:30:03.830 --> 00:30:06.135
so derkenen dem haynt, to

00:30:06.136 --> 00:30:08.629
understand day, daily life,

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:10.459
to explore this through surveys,

00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:12.739
interviews, to encourage

00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:14.329
personal documents such as

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:16.009
autobiographies.

00:30:16.010 --> 00:30:17.929
And I highly recommend

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:19.909
Oyerbakh's review of Shie Perle's Yidn

00:30:19.910 --> 00:30:22.099
fun a gants yor, Ordinary Jews,

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.469
which appeared in Literarishe bleter

00:30:24.470 --> 00:30:26.719
before the war, which really shows

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:28.669
Oyerbakh's closeness to the

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:30.739
ethos of the YIVO.

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:32.689
Indeed, the very structure of

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:34.879
Oyerbakh's wartime writings

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:36.799
emphasized the close links

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:38.539
of the Jewish intelligentsia and the

00:30:38.540 --> 00:30:39.799
masses.

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:41.779
The first title of her essays on

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:43.639
the murdered writers, musicians and

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:45.799
artists was "Tsuzamen mitn

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:47.989
folk," "Together With the People."

00:30:47.990 --> 00:30:49.819
Oyerbakh's work followed in the

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:52.369
footsteps not only of the Emanuel

00:30:52.370 --> 00:30:54.409
Ringelblum, but also of YIVO

00:30:54.410 --> 00:30:55.750
director Max Weinreich,

00:30:57.380 --> 00:30:59.089
who made special efforts to

00:30:59.090 --> 00:31:01.099
incorporate the insights

00:31:01.100 --> 00:31:02.989
of Sigmund Freud and of social

00:31:02.990 --> 00:31:04.999
psychology into the study

00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:06.000
of Polish Jewry.

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:08.749
In the highly politicized and

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.819
divided world of interwar Jewish

00:31:10.820 --> 00:31:13.069
Poland, where so many stressed

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:15.079
the primacy of the collective over the

00:31:15.080 --> 00:31:16.371
individual, Weinreich,

00:31:17.510 --> 00:31:19.759
in his youth studies, yugnt

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:21.919
forshn project, for instance,

00:31:21.920 --> 00:31:23.989
argued that one

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:26.029
could not understand Klal

00:31:26.030 --> 00:31:28.069
Yisroel, the collective,

00:31:28.070 --> 00:31:30.259
without grasping

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:32.419
the needs of Reb Yisroel,

00:31:32.420 --> 00:31:33.859
the individual.

00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:36.349
The aspirations, drives, obsessions,

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:38.779
hopes of the many individuals

00:31:38.780 --> 00:31:40.789
who made up Polish Jewry,

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:42.679
Oyerbakh, whose main field of

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:44.839
university study had

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:46.969
been psychology and who had written

00:31:46.970 --> 00:31:49.129
extensively on the field in

00:31:49.130 --> 00:31:51.439
the pre-war Jewish press, needed

00:31:51.440 --> 00:31:52.440
little convincing.

00:31:54.140 --> 00:31:57.169
And so, too, in her Holocaust

00:31:57.170 --> 00:31:59.509
writings, she highlighted,

00:31:59.510 --> 00:32:02.149
she highlighted the complex

00:32:02.150 --> 00:32:04.309
interplay of psychological

00:32:04.310 --> 00:32:06.979
factors in individuals,

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:09.799
families, entire social groups.

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:12.439
She told a complicated story

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:14.419
resilience, vitality,

00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:16.969
self-sacrifice, on the one hand,

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:19.099
corruption and moral collapse

00:32:19.100 --> 00:32:20.100
on the other.

00:32:21.050 --> 00:32:23.659
She also had brilliant insights,

00:32:23.660 --> 00:32:25.639
the result of conversations with

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:28.129
folklorist Shmuel Lehman in the ghetto

00:32:28.130 --> 00:32:30.529
about how rumors, jokes,

00:32:30.530 --> 00:32:32.569
street songs could

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:35.479
be seen as a self-defense reflex,

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:37.669
a poignant but powerful

00:32:37.670 --> 00:32:40.129
sign of the instinctive resistance

00:32:40.130 --> 00:32:41.329
of the Jewish masses.

00:32:42.390 --> 00:32:44.489
Oyerbakh stressed the German's

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:47.959
skillful use of psychological

00:32:47.960 --> 00:32:49.939
weaponry to effect

00:32:49.940 --> 00:32:51.949
the destruction of Warsaw

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:53.869
Jewry and to use the

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:56.149
Jews' strengths against

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:57.289
them.

00:32:57.290 --> 00:32:59.539
The Germans played with the Jews

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:02.089
and with their natural human

00:33:02.090 --> 00:33:04.519
instincts of self-preservation

00:33:04.520 --> 00:33:05.599
and hope.

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:07.549
In her brilliant observations

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:09.469
of the mass hysteria that gripped

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:11.659
the Warsaw ghetto in the summer

00:33:11.660 --> 00:33:13.699
of 1942

00:33:13.700 --> 00:33:15.709
to get into a shop, to bribe

00:33:15.710 --> 00:33:17.869
people to get a work certificate,

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:19.849
Oyerbakh described how the

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:21.799
very qualities that

00:33:21.800 --> 00:33:24.439
had served Jews so well in the past --

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:26.989
practicality, pragmatism,

00:33:26.990 --> 00:33:28.390
hard headedness, "seykhel,"

00:33:29.750 --> 00:33:31.849
and natural optimism --

00:33:31.850 --> 00:33:33.799
now accelerated their journey

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:34.800
into the abyss.

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:38.869
And now I quote" "and still

00:33:38.870 --> 00:33:41.479
other Jews broad-shouldered,

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:43.609
deep voiced with powerful

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:45.139
hands and hearts.

00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:47.299
Artisans, workers, wagon drivers,

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:49.249
porters, Jews who with the blow

00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:50.779
of their fists could floor any

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:52.849
hooligan who dared entered their

00:33:52.850 --> 00:33:54.259
neighborhoods.

00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:56.029
Where were you?" This is Leonard

00:33:56.030 --> 00:33:57.709
Wolf's translation, by the way.

00:33:57.710 --> 00:33:59.689
"Where were you when your

00:33:59.690 --> 00:34:01.759
wives and children, when your

00:34:01.760 --> 00:34:03.859
fathers and mothers were taken

00:34:03.860 --> 00:34:04.999
away?

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:06.889
What happened to make you run off like

00:34:06.890 --> 00:34:09.529
cattle stampeded by fire?

00:34:09.530 --> 00:34:11.209
Was there no one to give you some

00:34:11.210 --> 00:34:13.099
purpose in the confusion?

00:34:13.100 --> 00:34:15.229
You were swept away by the flood

00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:17.388
together with those who were weak

00:34:17.389 --> 00:34:19.819
and you sly, cunning merchants,

00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:21.769
philanthropists in your short fur

00:34:21.770 --> 00:34:22.880
coats and hats.

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:26.149
How was it that you didn't catch

00:34:26.150 --> 00:34:27.697
on to the murderous swindle?"

00:34:28.969 --> 00:34:30.799
Now, would other peoples confronted

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:32.928
with the similar massive assault,

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:34.158
have acted better?

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:36.019
She did not think so.

00:34:36.020 --> 00:34:38.329
Did Warsaw Jewry eventually recover

00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:40.129
from the shock and fight back?

00:34:40.130 --> 00:34:41.448
Yes.

00:34:41.449 --> 00:34:43.488
Should one blame the Jewish masses

00:34:43.489 --> 00:34:45.738
for not having fought back earlier?

00:34:45.739 --> 00:34:47.629
Only those who had not been there,

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:50.479
Oyerbakh implied, would do so.

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:52.428
Was armed resistance the only

00:34:52.429 --> 00:34:54.439
way that you stood up

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:55.579
to the Germans?

00:34:55.580 --> 00:34:56.647
Absolutely not.

00:34:58.969 --> 00:35:01.189
Oyerbakh was a superb observer

00:35:01.190 --> 00:35:02.929
of the Jewish everyday.

00:35:02.930 --> 00:35:05.299
As I said, she had a fine sense

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:07.249
of the nuances of

00:35:07.250 --> 00:35:09.049
the behavior and speech of different

00:35:09.050 --> 00:35:10.999
classes of Polish Jews,

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:12.979
whether they lived in small towns

00:35:12.980 --> 00:35:15.469
or in urban centers like Warsaw.

00:35:15.470 --> 00:35:17.389
Like Ringelblum, she was a native

00:35:17.390 --> 00:35:19.639
of southeast Galicia.

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:21.589
Before moving with her family to

00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:23.539
Lwow, she spent her childhood in

00:35:23.540 --> 00:35:26.029
a remote village, not a shtetl

00:35:26.030 --> 00:35:27.190
in Podolia, Lanowitz,

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:30.529
where she acquired a love of Jewish

00:35:30.530 --> 00:35:32.869
folklore and the Yiddish language.

00:35:32.870 --> 00:35:34.489
The great Jewish folklorist and

00:35:34.490 --> 00:35:36.379
ethnographer Shmuel Lehman had

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:38.329
interviewed her many times

00:35:38.330 --> 00:35:40.519
about the folk songs and customs

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:43.369
of the rural Podolian Jews.

00:35:43.370 --> 00:35:45.649
Like many other Galician Jews,

00:35:45.650 --> 00:35:47.629
Oyerbakh combined this deep

00:35:47.630 --> 00:35:49.849
Jewish identity with the first

00:35:49.850 --> 00:35:52.039
class Polish education cultural

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:54.199
sensibility at Lwow

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:56.809
University in the early 1920s,

00:35:56.810 --> 00:35:58.879
Oyerbakh befriended the young

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:00.829
poetist Debora Vogel and

00:36:00.830 --> 00:36:02.689
the budding writer and painter Bruno

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:04.759
Schulz, and she urged both

00:36:04.760 --> 00:36:06.709
to start writing in Yiddish

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:09.139
and Vogel indeed did so.

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:11.619
In 1929, she helped begin

00:36:11.620 --> 00:36:13.639
Tsushtayer a literary journal

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:14.599
that tried to bring the

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:16.879
Polish-speaking Galician Jewish

00:36:16.880 --> 00:36:18.860
intelligentsia closer to Yiddish.

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:22.329
Oyerbakh would go on to have many

00:36:22.330 --> 00:36:24.159
interests, but one of the most salient

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:26.289
was her devotion from the very young

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:28.329
age to get her secular

00:36:28.330 --> 00:36:29.289
culture.

00:36:29.290 --> 00:36:30.969
Her vision of that culture was

00:36:30.970 --> 00:36:33.219
inclusive rather than exclusive,

00:36:33.220 --> 00:36:35.859
one that united rather than divided.

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:37.389
That transcended political

00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:39.399
differences, a culture whose

00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:41.199
creativity would shame those who

00:36:41.200 --> 00:36:43.299
regarded Yiddish as nothing more

00:36:43.300 --> 00:36:45.249
than a low class jargon.

00:36:45.250 --> 00:36:47.229
But in the end, Tsushtayer

00:36:47.230 --> 00:36:49.089
was a bridge too far.

00:36:49.090 --> 00:36:51.279
It folded in 1932 after

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:53.199
three issues, unable

00:36:53.200 --> 00:36:55.749
to overcome financial difficulties

00:36:55.750 --> 00:36:58.299
and disagreements over politics.

00:36:58.300 --> 00:37:00.099
For Oyerbakh, Yiddish culture came

00:37:00.100 --> 00:37:01.539
first, politics second.

00:37:01.540 --> 00:37:02.699
But others disagreed.

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:05.019
And one more thing.

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:08.979
As she started the journal,

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:11.259
she had to endure some condescending

00:37:11.260 --> 00:37:13.479
and misogynistic sniping

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:15.100
aimed at her and at Dvora Vogel.

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:18.159
This might have been the first such

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:19.959
incident in her life, but it was

00:37:19.960 --> 00:37:21.879
definitely not the last.

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:24.519
In the late 1930s in Warsaw,

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:26.229
she earned the nickname "di

00:37:26.230 --> 00:37:27.230
literarishe baylage,"

00:37:28.360 --> 00:37:30.279
"the literary supplement,"

00:37:30.280 --> 00:37:32.529
a snide reference to her relationship

00:37:32.530 --> 00:37:33.530
with Itzik Manger.

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:36.309
And later on, she would wonder

00:37:36.310 --> 00:37:38.469
if her path in Yad Vashem

00:37:38.470 --> 00:37:40.509
would have been easier had

00:37:40.510 --> 00:37:42.369
she not been a woman.

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:44.379
But that only encouraged Oyerbakh

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:46.299
to redouble her efforts to

00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:48.489
highlight the critical contributions

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:50.499
of women to Yiddish literature.

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:54.139
Despite her love of Galicia

00:37:54.140 --> 00:37:56.149
and of Lwow

00:37:56.150 --> 00:37:58.099
she realized the Yiddish high

00:37:58.100 --> 00:38:00.409
culture faced a bleak future there.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:03.169
In the 1930s, she moved, in 1932,

00:38:03.170 --> 00:38:04.909
she moved to Warsaw.

00:38:04.910 --> 00:38:07.009
After her brief marriage to the poet

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:09.289
Ber Schnapper failed, she became

00:38:09.290 --> 00:38:11.329
the companion of the brilliant

00:38:11.330 --> 00:38:13.609
and tempestuous Yiddish poet

00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:16.009
Itzik Manger, and supported

00:38:16.010 --> 00:38:18.709
herself precari-- precariously

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:20.749
by translation and by writing

00:38:20.750 --> 00:38:22.729
articles and reviews for the

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:25.020
Jewish press in Polish and Yiddish.

00:38:26.270 --> 00:38:28.549
Much of the power of Oyerbakh's

00:38:28.550 --> 00:38:30.769
reportage stem from her ability

00:38:30.770 --> 00:38:33.439
to navigate cultural boundaries

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:35.539
derived from a life that straddled

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:37.759
many different worlds village,

00:38:37.760 --> 00:38:38.689
shtetl, and city.

00:38:38.690 --> 00:38:41.509
Yiddish and Polish, Poles and Jews.

00:38:41.510 --> 00:38:43.469
Galicia and Warsaw, the milieu

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:45.469
of the Jewish literary elite

00:38:45.470 --> 00:38:47.330
and the world of the Jewish masses.

00:38:48.390 --> 00:38:50.489
Religious and secular.

00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:52.949
Diaspora nationalists and Zionists,

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:53.950
she straddled them all.

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:58.559
At the end of September 1939

00:38:58.560 --> 00:39:00.899
after Poland's capitulation,

00:39:00.900 --> 00:39:03.719
Oyerbakh was about to flee to Lwow

00:39:03.720 --> 00:39:05.609
when she received an unexpected

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:08.069
summons from Emmanuel Ringelblum

00:39:08.070 --> 00:39:09.749
to stay in Warsaw and run a soup

00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:11.699
kitchen. Again, I'm basing this on

00:39:11.700 --> 00:39:13.349
what she wrote.

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:15.299
Most of the Jewish elite had

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:17.519
run away from Warsaw, but Rinelblum

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:19.499
had reminded Oyerbakh that

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:21.780
not everyone had the right to run.

00:39:23.440 --> 00:39:25.539
Ringelblum thus brought Oyerbakh into

00:39:25.540 --> 00:39:27.249
the far flung network of the

00:39:27.250 --> 00:39:28.250
self-help, the Aleynhilf

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:31.059
of the Warsaw Ghetto.

00:39:31.060 --> 00:39:33.099
With its hundreds of house committees,

00:39:33.100 --> 00:39:35.049
dozens of soup kitchens, day care

00:39:35.050 --> 00:39:37.179
centers and

00:39:37.180 --> 00:39:39.309
refugee shelters,

00:39:39.310 --> 00:39:41.379
the leaders of the Aleynhilf saw the

00:39:41.380 --> 00:39:43.599
organization as the conscience

00:39:43.600 --> 00:39:45.999
of Warsaw Jewry, a counterweight

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:47.349
to the Judenrat.

00:39:47.350 --> 00:39:49.389
During World War One, the Yiddish

00:39:49.390 --> 00:39:51.489
writer Yitzhak Leybush Peretz

00:39:51.490 --> 00:39:54.399
had stressed the interdependence

00:39:54.400 --> 00:39:56.979
of relief and zamling,

00:39:56.980 --> 00:39:59.049
the recording of history and

00:39:59.050 --> 00:40:00.249
in the Second World War.

00:40:00.250 --> 00:40:02.799
By the same token, the Aleynhilf

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:04.514
made the functioning of the Oyneg

00:40:04.515 --> 00:40:06.939
Shabes archive possible.

00:40:06.940 --> 00:40:09.069
Ringelblum used the soup kitchens and

00:40:09.070 --> 00:40:11.649
refugee centers to gather information,

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:13.509
to camouflage the gathering of

00:40:13.510 --> 00:40:16.269
information and to find writers.

00:40:16.270 --> 00:40:18.309
And at the same time, by giving

00:40:18.310 --> 00:40:20.439
them jobs, he tried to save the

00:40:20.440 --> 00:40:22.689
cadres of the Jewish intelligentsia.

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:24.699
In 1941, he

00:40:24.700 --> 00:40:27.399
recruited Oyerbakh to the Oyneg Shabes

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:29.559
by asking her to write about the soup

00:40:29.560 --> 00:40:31.719
kitchen. And Oyerbakh would stress

00:40:31.720 --> 00:40:33.909
that had it not been for Ringelblum,

00:40:33.910 --> 00:40:36.369
she would not have written anything.

00:40:36.370 --> 00:40:39.069
Oyerbakh's Soup Kitchen on Leszno

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:41.739
40, which fed 2000

00:40:41.740 --> 00:40:42.740
Jews a day,

00:40:44.290 --> 00:40:46.419
offered her a unique vantage

00:40:46.420 --> 00:40:48.489
point to get to know and

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:50.619
observe a large number of,

00:40:50.620 --> 00:40:52.210
quote unquote, customers.

00:40:53.530 --> 00:40:55.929
The story that Oyerbakh told

00:40:55.930 --> 00:40:57.849
provided a gripping behind the

00:40:57.850 --> 00:41:00.249
scenes look at the desperate

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:02.229
battle that the ghetto was waging

00:41:02.230 --> 00:41:03.789
against hunger.

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:05.949
Oyerbakh investigated

00:41:05.950 --> 00:41:08.229
the social history of hunger

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:10.509
by telling the story of a soup kitchen

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:13.209
as a microcosm of human relationships

00:41:13.210 --> 00:41:14.949
and human choices.

00:41:14.950 --> 00:41:16.959
The dead who ended up in the pits of

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:19.029
the Gensha cemetery, were

00:41:19.030 --> 00:41:21.249
not an undifferentiated mass,

00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:24.279
but individuals whose idiosyncrasies

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:26.109
Oyerbakh recorded.

00:41:26.110 --> 00:41:28.539
She deftly caught the phrases

00:41:28.540 --> 00:41:30.699
or the habits that made these

00:41:30.700 --> 00:41:32.649
individuals memorable.

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:34.719
The young mother expelled

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:37.119
from her shtetl, who arrived

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:39.309
at the soup kitchen with four children

00:41:39.310 --> 00:41:41.259
in tow and a tot

00:41:41.260 --> 00:41:43.359
suckling at her breast, a

00:41:43.360 --> 00:41:45.579
family of solid, rugged Jewish

00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:47.919
boys from a country farm

00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:50.239
who had just been expelled.

00:41:50.240 --> 00:41:52.269
And each day they take a bowl of

00:41:52.270 --> 00:41:54.129
soup for their aged father.

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:55.929
And whenever they mentioned him, they

00:41:55.930 --> 00:41:58.449
never failed to add "biz hundert un

00:41:58.450 --> 00:42:00.659
tsvantsig," "may he live to 120."

00:42:01.720 --> 00:42:03.999
Many solid members

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:05.949
of the Jewish middle class now

00:42:05.950 --> 00:42:08.409
reduced to desperate poverty.

00:42:08.410 --> 00:42:10.749
Oyerbakh, as the director

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:13.539
of the soup kitchen, confronted

00:42:13.540 --> 00:42:15.729
serious psychological pressures

00:42:15.730 --> 00:42:17.349
and moral challenges.

00:42:17.350 --> 00:42:19.809
Like other figures of the Aleynhilf,

00:42:19.810 --> 00:42:21.879
she had seen herself

00:42:21.880 --> 00:42:24.129
as an honest public servant

00:42:24.130 --> 00:42:26.259
imbued with the moral code

00:42:26.260 --> 00:42:27.519
of the progressive Jewish

00:42:27.520 --> 00:42:28.839
intelligentsia.

00:42:28.840 --> 00:42:30.819
But very quickly, she discovered that

00:42:30.820 --> 00:42:32.919
for the masses of starving, desperate

00:42:32.920 --> 00:42:35.049
people who came to the kitchen each

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:37.599
day, she represented authority

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:38.679
and power.

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:40.359
In their eyes, she had become an

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:42.219
arbiter of life and death.

00:42:42.220 --> 00:42:44.319
Like Ringelblum, she had

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:46.239
come from a militia that was

00:42:46.240 --> 00:42:48.579
imbued with love for, quote unquote,

00:42:48.580 --> 00:42:50.409
the Jewish masses.

00:42:50.410 --> 00:42:52.359
But now these masses, those

00:42:52.360 --> 00:42:55.119
ordinary Jews, stormed her office,

00:42:55.120 --> 00:42:57.099
blocked her in the corridors and

00:42:57.100 --> 00:42:59.709
begged for extra soup tickets.

00:42:59.710 --> 00:43:01.509
They looked at Oyerbakh as someone who

00:43:01.510 --> 00:43:02.409
could save them.

00:43:02.410 --> 00:43:03.906
Some confronted her in silent

00:43:03.907 --> 00:43:04.907
supplication.

00:43:05.290 --> 00:43:07.539
Others yelled and loudly

00:43:07.540 --> 00:43:10.149
reminded her of their former status.

00:43:10.150 --> 00:43:12.189
Then there were the children who would

00:43:12.190 --> 00:43:14.499
come in packs and cajole

00:43:14.500 --> 00:43:16.989
her in Yiddish accented Polish

00:43:16.990 --> 00:43:19.149
prosze pani, prosze, pani for

00:43:19.150 --> 00:43:20.949
some extra food.

00:43:20.950 --> 00:43:23.379
And it seemed to her then,

00:43:23.380 --> 00:43:25.359
as if the kitchen only prolonged

00:43:25.360 --> 00:43:27.489
the agony but really saved no

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:29.889
one. Should she favor a chosen

00:43:29.890 --> 00:43:31.809
few or dole out

00:43:31.810 --> 00:43:34.449
equal rations and save no one?

00:43:34.450 --> 00:43:37.149
Ringelblum asked the same question.

00:43:37.150 --> 00:43:39.519
Increasingly frustrated,

00:43:39.520 --> 00:43:42.159
Oyerbakh singled out a chosen

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:44.949
few for special care.

00:43:44.950 --> 00:43:46.959
The religious poet Israel Shtern,

00:43:46.960 --> 00:43:49.239
for instance, or a German

00:43:49.240 --> 00:43:51.339
speaking refugee from the

00:43:51.340 --> 00:43:53.679
Sudetenland who had survived Dachau

00:43:53.680 --> 00:43:55.539
and whom Ringelblum wanted to keep

00:43:55.540 --> 00:43:57.279
alive as a witness.

00:43:57.280 --> 00:43:59.199
This former athlete,

00:43:59.200 --> 00:44:01.059
the Daytsch, as he was called in

00:44:01.060 --> 00:44:03.519
Warsaw Yiddish, won Oyerbakh

00:44:03.520 --> 00:44:05.469
over with his impeccable

00:44:05.470 --> 00:44:07.599
manners, his good nature,

00:44:07.600 --> 00:44:09.759
his spry sense of humor.

00:44:09.760 --> 00:44:11.829
For Oyerbakh, Brockmeyer

00:44:11.830 --> 00:44:14.619
became a litmus test and a challenge

00:44:14.620 --> 00:44:16.749
she bent her rules and gave him extra

00:44:16.750 --> 00:44:18.669
food. When he began to become

00:44:18.670 --> 00:44:20.649
apathetic, a common sign of

00:44:20.650 --> 00:44:22.629
starvation, she would yell at

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:24.739
him that she could feed him, but

00:44:24.740 --> 00:44:26.679
that he had to summon up the will to

00:44:26.680 --> 00:44:28.839
live. It was important Oyerbakh,

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:30.909
though, to win at least one

00:44:30.910 --> 00:44:32.109
victory.

00:44:32.110 --> 00:44:34.209
But here, too, she failed.

00:44:34.210 --> 00:44:36.369
In her diary entry of

00:44:36.370 --> 00:44:38.289
August 4th, 1941, she

00:44:38.290 --> 00:44:40.389
noted that "what is the use

00:44:40.390 --> 00:44:42.429
of all our work if we can't save

00:44:42.430 --> 00:44:44.349
even one person from death

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:46.449
by hunger?" Ringelblum

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:48.459
soon gave her new assignments.

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:50.859
In September 1942,

00:44:50.860 --> 00:44:52.959
he and Shmuel Winter asked Oyerbakh

00:44:52.960 --> 00:44:54.969
to interview Avrom Krzepicki, who

00:44:54.970 --> 00:44:57.069
just escaped from Treblinka.

00:44:57.070 --> 00:44:58.629
And there was a protocol of more than

00:44:58.630 --> 00:45:01.509
100 pages written by Oyerbakh.

00:45:01.510 --> 00:45:03.549
Krzepicki did not survive,

00:45:03.550 --> 00:45:05.919
but after the war, Oyerbakh developed

00:45:05.920 --> 00:45:07.749
a special bond with the Treblinka

00:45:07.750 --> 00:45:09.729
survivors, about whom she

00:45:09.730 --> 00:45:11.799
wrote in the late 1940s

00:45:11.800 --> 00:45:13.659
and whose honor she defended in the

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:16.239
1960s during the controversy

00:45:16.240 --> 00:45:18.279
over Steiner's book.

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:20.409
In February 1943, she

00:45:20.410 --> 00:45:22.539
escaped to the Aryan side

00:45:22.540 --> 00:45:24.609
and helped by Polish friends,

00:45:24.610 --> 00:45:26.739
she procured Ayran documents

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:28.329
and became a courier for the Jewish

00:45:28.330 --> 00:45:29.330
underground.

00:45:29.980 --> 00:45:32.169
On the Aryan side, after

00:45:32.170 --> 00:45:34.359
the ghetto uprising, Oyerbakh

00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:36.429
wrote a constant stream of essays for

00:45:36.430 --> 00:45:38.439
what she called arkhiv hemshekh,

00:45:38.440 --> 00:45:40.569
the continuation archive.

00:45:40.570 --> 00:45:42.729
With the help of Polish friends,

00:45:42.730 --> 00:45:44.799
Oyerbakh buried her writings in two

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:47.079
different locations in Warsaw

00:45:47.080 --> 00:45:49.189
on the grounds of the zoo and

00:45:49.190 --> 00:45:50.949
then a field in the southern district

00:45:50.950 --> 00:45:51.969
of Mokotow.

00:45:51.970 --> 00:45:54.099
Both caches survived the war.

00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:56.469
As Oyerbakh wrote, "I had better luck

00:45:56.470 --> 00:45:58.539
in saving paper than

00:45:58.540 --> 00:45:59.580
in saving people."

00:46:01.240 --> 00:46:03.189
One of the greatest of her wartime

00:46:03.190 --> 00:46:05.559
works, one of the very few translated

00:46:05.560 --> 00:46:08.619
into English, was "Yizkor,"

00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:10.659
first written, she said,

00:46:10.660 --> 00:46:13.239
in late 1943.

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:14.769
And we'll hear more about this from

00:46:14.770 --> 00:46:15.969
David Roskies.

00:46:15.970 --> 00:46:18.489
Sitting on a Warsaw trolley car,

00:46:18.490 --> 00:46:20.799
she'd seen a Polish woman weeping

00:46:20.800 --> 00:46:22.719
over her son, who the Germans

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:24.634
had shot, and Oyerbakh realized

00:46:26.350 --> 00:46:28.419
that she didn't have

00:46:28.420 --> 00:46:30.939
the luxury of openly mourning.

00:46:30.940 --> 00:46:33.189
And so she went back to her apartment,

00:46:33.190 --> 00:46:35.169
so she wrote, and began

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:37.189
her great elegy for Warsaw Jewry.

00:46:37.190 --> 00:46:39.399
"Yizkor, I may neither groan

00:46:39.400 --> 00:46:41.409
nor weep. I may not draw attention

00:46:41.410 --> 00:46:43.419
to myself in the street and I need to

00:46:43.420 --> 00:46:45.339
groan. I need to weep.

00:46:45.340 --> 00:46:47.049
Not four times a year.

00:46:47.050 --> 00:46:48.909
I feel the need to say Yizkor four

00:46:48.910 --> 00:46:49.689
times a day.".

00:46:49.690 --> 00:46:51.789
Yizkor is the service of remembrance,

00:46:51.790 --> 00:46:53.679
a prayer of remembrance.

00:46:53.680 --> 00:46:55.839
She poured out her soul

00:46:55.840 --> 00:46:57.759
as she tried to describe the murder

00:46:57.760 --> 00:46:59.229
of Warsaw Jewry.

00:46:59.230 --> 00:47:01.029
The toddlers, the children who she

00:47:01.030 --> 00:47:03.159
remembered from the ghetto schools,

00:47:03.160 --> 00:47:05.319
the tough Jewish workers, the hardened

00:47:05.320 --> 00:47:07.449
women shopkeepers, young scouts,

00:47:07.450 --> 00:47:10.149
courting couples, intellectuals

00:47:10.150 --> 00:47:11.589
all gone.

00:47:11.590 --> 00:47:14.379
Even if a few individuals survived,

00:47:14.380 --> 00:47:16.479
the vibrant, raucous and diverse

00:47:16.480 --> 00:47:18.309
mosaic of Warsaw Jewry had been

00:47:18.310 --> 00:47:19.449
destroyed.

00:47:19.450 --> 00:47:21.849
"Yizkor" humanized the victims

00:47:21.850 --> 00:47:23.709
by recalling not only their

00:47:23.710 --> 00:47:25.659
individuality but also their

00:47:25.660 --> 00:47:28.179
city, the specific urban

00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:30.129
milieu that had shaped them and

00:47:30.130 --> 00:47:31.130
that had made them Varshever.

00:47:32.110 --> 00:47:34.179
In another essay she wrote,

00:47:34.180 --> 00:47:36.249
Oyerbakh indeed regretted the lack

00:47:36.250 --> 00:47:38.199
of armed resistance until January

00:47:38.200 --> 00:47:39.819
1943.

00:47:39.820 --> 00:47:41.649
But "Yizkor" was different.

00:47:41.650 --> 00:47:43.539
Unlike the works of some other Oyneg

00:47:43.540 --> 00:47:45.489
Shabes writers who observe the

00:47:45.490 --> 00:47:47.410
deportation like Yoshue

00:47:47.411 --> 00:47:49.449
Perle, Israel Liechtenstein

00:47:49.450 --> 00:47:50.729
or Perez Opocynski, Oyerbakh's

00:47:52.780 --> 00:47:55.869
"Yizkor" was suffused with empathy

00:47:55.870 --> 00:47:58.089
towards Jews rather than anger,

00:47:58.090 --> 00:48:00.609
as David Roskies has pointed out,

00:48:00.610 --> 00:48:02.739
by using the imagery of a flood,

00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:04.959
of a natural disaster at the beginning

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:07.239
of "Yizkor." She anticipated

00:48:07.240 --> 00:48:09.249
future questions that those

00:48:09.250 --> 00:48:11.469
who were not there would pose.

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:14.199
No, the mass murder was not a

00:48:14.200 --> 00:48:16.149
meta-historical event, but

00:48:16.150 --> 00:48:18.279
its enormity was too horrible

00:48:18.280 --> 00:48:20.409
and to unprecedented to allow

00:48:20.410 --> 00:48:23.049
for glib theories and facile questions

00:48:23.050 --> 00:48:25.209
that might compromise the memory

00:48:25.210 --> 00:48:27.189
of the ordinary Jews she cherished

00:48:27.190 --> 00:48:29.019
so deeply.

00:48:29.020 --> 00:48:31.029
And how does one resist the

00:48:31.030 --> 00:48:33.909
flood or an earthquake?

00:48:33.910 --> 00:48:35.919
What befell the Jewish masses was

00:48:35.920 --> 00:48:38.409
so unthinkable and so calamitous

00:48:38.410 --> 00:48:39.819
that they were psychologically

00:48:39.820 --> 00:48:41.049
unprepared.

00:48:41.050 --> 00:48:44.109
Implicitly, Oyerbakh was anticipating

00:48:44.110 --> 00:48:46.089
the invidious distinctions that many

00:48:46.090 --> 00:48:48.549
would make after the war between

00:48:48.550 --> 00:48:51.219
the few who fought back with weapons

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:53.319
and the masses that allegedly died

00:48:53.320 --> 00:48:54.489
without a fight.

00:48:54.490 --> 00:48:56.299
It was the people she wrote about in

00:48:56.300 --> 00:48:58.269
"Yizkor," not the fighters who'd

00:48:58.270 --> 00:49:00.399
written up, who'd risen up just months

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:01.599
before.

00:49:01.600 --> 00:49:03.549
And in the end, a secular writer could

00:49:03.550 --> 00:49:05.109
only repeat the words of the

00:49:05.110 --> 00:49:07.179
traditional Yizkor prayer.

00:49:07.180 --> 00:49:09.159
For Oyerbakh, Yizkor

00:49:09.160 --> 00:49:11.349
was a return not to religion,

00:49:11.350 --> 00:49:12.819
but to the murdered world of her

00:49:12.820 --> 00:49:15.099
birth, to a folk community

00:49:15.100 --> 00:49:17.409
that did not have to invent new words

00:49:17.410 --> 00:49:19.299
to describe pain and loss.

00:49:19.300 --> 00:49:20.829
They were already there in the

00:49:20.830 --> 00:49:21.759
prayers.

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:23.969
Like her murdered friends, Oyerbakh

00:49:23.970 --> 00:49:25.979
was "tsuzamen mitn folk,"

00:49:25.980 --> 00:49:26.980
together with the people.

00:49:28.830 --> 00:49:30.869
One of the more striking

00:49:30.870 --> 00:49:32.819
features of Oyerbakh's writings

00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:35.009
of this period is her portrayal

00:49:35.010 --> 00:49:37.079
of Poland and the Poles.

00:49:37.080 --> 00:49:39.269
As a courier who distributed money

00:49:39.270 --> 00:49:41.789
and aid to hidden Jews, Oyerbakh

00:49:41.790 --> 00:49:43.819
was in constant contact with

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:45.929
the category very small

00:49:45.930 --> 00:49:48.689
in number, of Poles

00:49:48.690 --> 00:49:50.699
who most Jews during the war never had

00:49:50.700 --> 00:49:52.589
a chance to meet the liberal

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:54.569
intellectual elite.

00:49:54.570 --> 00:49:56.759
Her descriptions of such individuals

00:49:56.760 --> 00:49:58.889
as Jan Zabinski and his wife

00:49:58.890 --> 00:50:00.599
are particularly instructive and

00:50:00.600 --> 00:50:01.799
insightful.

00:50:01.800 --> 00:50:03.659
Zabinski had been the director of the

00:50:03.660 --> 00:50:05.699
Warsaw Zoo, as well

00:50:05.700 --> 00:50:06.989
as an officer in the Polish

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:07.979
underground.

00:50:07.980 --> 00:50:09.929
During the war, he and his wife had

00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:11.789
helped many Jews, including Oyerbakh

00:50:11.790 --> 00:50:12.790
herself.

00:50:13.590 --> 00:50:15.599
Her intense devotion to the memory

00:50:15.600 --> 00:50:18.059
of Jewish Warsaw in no way

00:50:18.060 --> 00:50:20.009
diminished her deep empathy for

00:50:20.010 --> 00:50:22.829
Polish Warsaw and its sufferings.

00:50:22.830 --> 00:50:23.830
In Varshever Tsavoes,

00:50:24.840 --> 00:50:27.209
she painted a sympathetic portrait

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:29.195
of Warsaw's Mayor Stefan Starzynski,

00:50:30.330 --> 00:50:32.309
who called out his fellow citizens to

00:50:32.310 --> 00:50:34.259
resist the Germans and

00:50:34.260 --> 00:50:36.029
who led the city during the days of

00:50:36.030 --> 00:50:38.673
the terrible siege in September 1939.

00:50:39.870 --> 00:50:42.449
Right after a terrible bombardment

00:50:42.450 --> 00:50:44.399
of Jewish neighborhoods,

00:50:44.400 --> 00:50:47.279
Starzynski paid a visit

00:50:47.280 --> 00:50:48.959
and stroked the heads of Jewish

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:49.949
children.

00:50:49.950 --> 00:50:52.259
Perhaps, Oyerbakh speculated

00:50:52.260 --> 00:50:53.819
as he walked through those Jewish

00:50:53.820 --> 00:50:55.949
streets, Starzynski was thinking

00:50:55.950 --> 00:50:58.079
how, before the war, Poland

00:50:58.080 --> 00:50:59.909
had treated its Jews as second class

00:50:59.910 --> 00:51:00.809
citizens.

00:51:00.810 --> 00:51:02.759
At least that's what she wrote.

00:51:02.760 --> 00:51:04.739
Perhaps the terror of the siege had

00:51:04.740 --> 00:51:06.299
one silver lining.

00:51:06.300 --> 00:51:08.229
For a brief moment, the fight against

00:51:08.230 --> 00:51:10.169
the common German enemy brought

00:51:10.170 --> 00:51:12.389
Poles and Jews together and offered

00:51:12.390 --> 00:51:14.339
another glimpse into the heart of what

00:51:14.340 --> 00:51:15.839
might have been.

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:16.840
Before the war, Starzynski

00:51:17.970 --> 00:51:19.919
had, to put it mildly,

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:21.140
he'd not been known as a philosemite.

00:51:22.590 --> 00:51:24.509
But like so many others, Oyerbakh

00:51:24.510 --> 00:51:26.639
speculated, perhaps the war

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:28.690
caused him to grow beyond himself.

00:51:30.060 --> 00:51:31.060
Many people,

00:51:32.160 --> 00:51:34.259
Oyerbakh noted, would

00:51:34.260 --> 00:51:36.389
wonder why

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:37.679
Starzynski and his fellow Poles

00:51:37.680 --> 00:51:41.189
defended Warsaw in September 1939.

00:51:41.190 --> 00:51:43.259
It was such a hopeless fight that led

00:51:43.260 --> 00:51:45.359
to so many needless civilian

00:51:45.360 --> 00:51:46.409
casualties.

00:51:46.410 --> 00:51:48.659
Another Warsaw Jewish diarist, Chaim

00:51:48.660 --> 00:51:50.939
Kaplan, had nothing but contempt

00:51:50.940 --> 00:51:52.859
for a doomed battle that caused

00:51:52.860 --> 00:51:54.209
so much suffering.

00:51:54.210 --> 00:51:56.279
But Oyerbakh, nurtured on

00:51:56.280 --> 00:51:58.289
Polish romanticism, was full of

00:51:58.290 --> 00:52:00.359
admiration and I quote,

00:52:00.360 --> 00:52:03.299
" Romanticism, idealism.

00:52:03.300 --> 00:52:05.519
Let the naysayers get the contempt

00:52:05.520 --> 00:52:06.659
they deserve.

00:52:06.660 --> 00:52:08.699
Other Europeans peoples

00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:09.959
did not fight.

00:52:09.960 --> 00:52:12.449
But we Polish Jews understand

00:52:12.450 --> 00:52:14.099
what the Poles did.

00:52:14.100 --> 00:52:16.199
We were at their side and

00:52:16.200 --> 00:52:18.629
we understand today."

00:52:18.630 --> 00:52:20.639
Now, Oyerbakh's relationship to

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:22.709
Poland was paradoxical

00:52:22.710 --> 00:52:24.059
and complicated.

00:52:24.060 --> 00:52:25.949
And hopefully we will hear more about

00:52:25.950 --> 00:52:27.989
the subject later on, especially from

00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:28.990
Karolina.

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:31.559
Oyerbakh the Yiddishist actually

00:52:31.560 --> 00:52:33.209
did most of her wartime writing in

00:52:33.210 --> 00:52:35.219
Polish and in her

00:52:35.220 --> 00:52:37.100
book of reportage, In Land Yisorel,

00:52:38.310 --> 00:52:40.379
she spoke about Jews leaving

00:52:40.380 --> 00:52:42.299
Poland after the

00:52:42.300 --> 00:52:44.369
war for Israel, carrying

00:52:44.370 --> 00:52:46.199
Jastrun's biography of Adam

00:52:46.200 --> 00:52:47.200
Mickiewicz.

00:52:47.340 --> 00:52:49.319
And in that same book she

00:52:49.320 --> 00:52:51.329
wrote about the final days before

00:52:51.330 --> 00:52:52.829
she left Poland forever

00:52:53.880 --> 00:52:55.019
to go to Israel.

00:52:55.020 --> 00:52:56.939
And she remembered that

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:59.219
when her grandparents had left Galicia

00:52:59.220 --> 00:53:01.319
before World War One, they

00:53:01.320 --> 00:53:03.449
had spent months going

00:53:03.450 --> 00:53:05.369
from shtetl to shtetl,

00:53:05.370 --> 00:53:08.279
saying goodbye to multiple relatives.

00:53:08.280 --> 00:53:10.229
Now she was leaving Poland and

00:53:10.230 --> 00:53:12.119
she didn't have one relative to say

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:14.699
goodbye to. She'd lost everyone.

00:53:14.700 --> 00:53:16.859
But whom did she visit?

00:53:16.860 --> 00:53:18.959
She spent those final days

00:53:18.960 --> 00:53:20.969
with the Polish family who

00:53:20.970 --> 00:53:22.949
had been her childhood neighbors

00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:24.809
when she'd lived in Lanovtsy as a

00:53:24.810 --> 00:53:26.759
young girl and who, after the

00:53:26.760 --> 00:53:29.009
war, had moved

00:53:29.010 --> 00:53:31.139
to the former German territories

00:53:31.140 --> 00:53:32.279
in the West.

00:53:32.280 --> 00:53:34.589
Her description of that visit,

00:53:34.590 --> 00:53:35.590
that kind of,

00:53:36.970 --> 00:53:39.129
end of of a chapter

00:53:39.130 --> 00:53:40.130
in her life

00:53:41.050 --> 00:53:43.209
underscored the close bonds

00:53:43.210 --> 00:53:45.249
that she felt had existed between

00:53:45.250 --> 00:53:47.529
Poles and Jews in that distant

00:53:47.530 --> 00:53:49.239
rural past.

00:53:49.240 --> 00:53:51.279
And of course, Oyerbakh was no fool.

00:53:51.280 --> 00:53:53.439
During the time she was in Poland,

00:53:53.440 --> 00:53:55.899
about 1500 Jews were murdered

00:53:55.900 --> 00:53:58.209
by, by Poles.

00:53:58.210 --> 00:54:00.399
And I'm not saying

00:54:00.400 --> 00:54:02.319
her attitude towards Poland was one of

00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:05.079
unalloyed admiration,

00:54:05.080 --> 00:54:06.579
but there was certainly an element of

00:54:06.580 --> 00:54:07.599
paradox here.

00:54:08.710 --> 00:54:10.749
In the tension between her wartime

00:54:10.750 --> 00:54:12.429
writings and

00:54:14.050 --> 00:54:16.029
her postwar emendations.

00:54:16.030 --> 00:54:18.459
One can discern a marked shift

00:54:18.460 --> 00:54:20.469
not just in subject matter, but in

00:54:20.470 --> 00:54:22.479
emphasis and attitude.

00:54:22.480 --> 00:54:24.789
In her wartime writings and reportage

00:54:24.790 --> 00:54:26.949
for the archive, Oyerbakh

00:54:26.950 --> 00:54:28.539
judged herself harshly.

00:54:28.540 --> 00:54:30.699
The soup kitchen had been a failure.

00:54:30.700 --> 00:54:32.499
The Aleynhilf, an exercise in

00:54:32.500 --> 00:54:33.759
futility.

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:35.769
But after the war, she realized

00:54:35.770 --> 00:54:37.539
the kitchens had been more effective

00:54:37.540 --> 00:54:38.540
than she thought,

00:54:39.670 --> 00:54:41.649
and that Polish Yiddish culture, as

00:54:41.650 --> 00:54:43.539
I said, had a future.

00:54:43.540 --> 00:54:45.819
The same pattern of self-criticism,

00:54:45.820 --> 00:54:48.309
followed by positive reevaluation,

00:54:48.310 --> 00:54:50.799
emerged in other postwar writings.

00:54:50.800 --> 00:54:52.659
After the war, she joined the Central

00:54:52.660 --> 00:54:54.219
Jewish Historical Commission in

00:54:54.220 --> 00:54:56.199
Poland, where Philip

00:54:56.200 --> 00:54:58.029
Friedman, Joseph Kermisz, Michal

00:54:58.030 --> 00:55:00.189
Borwicz and others were busily

00:55:00.190 --> 00:55:02.199
documenting the Holocaust and

00:55:02.200 --> 00:55:04.509
publishing the first serious studies.

00:55:04.510 --> 00:55:06.369
At the time, she focused on the

00:55:06.370 --> 00:55:08.349
defects, rather the achievements of

00:55:08.350 --> 00:55:10.179
the, of the historical commission.

00:55:12.730 --> 00:55:13.730
But,

00:55:15.370 --> 00:55:17.679
and she thought

00:55:17.680 --> 00:55:20.559
that they were falling short,

00:55:20.560 --> 00:55:22.509
but in Israel in the 1950s,

00:55:22.510 --> 00:55:24.639
she changed her tune.

00:55:24.640 --> 00:55:26.499
Compared to the paltry record of

00:55:26.500 --> 00:55:28.719
scholarship on the Holocaust in Israel

00:55:28.720 --> 00:55:30.609
and elsewhere in the 1950s.

00:55:30.610 --> 00:55:32.529
She now wrote that postwar Poland

00:55:32.530 --> 00:55:34.479
had been a golden age of Holocaust

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:35.859
research.

00:55:35.860 --> 00:55:38.019
In Israel at Yad Vashem, her single

00:55:38.020 --> 00:55:40.089
minded determination to foreground

00:55:40.090 --> 00:55:42.429
Holocaust research and the collection

00:55:42.430 --> 00:55:44.469
of survivor testimony caused

00:55:44.470 --> 00:55:46.719
major conflicts, which Boaz Cohen

00:55:46.720 --> 00:55:48.039
will talk about.

00:55:48.040 --> 00:55:50.079
It is sad but true that her last

00:55:50.080 --> 00:55:52.869
years in Israel were not happy ones.

00:55:52.870 --> 00:55:54.579
Oyerbakh, to put it mildly, was not an

00:55:54.580 --> 00:55:56.469
easy person to get along with.

00:55:56.470 --> 00:55:58.509
And I sense that in her last years

00:55:58.510 --> 00:56:00.189
she felt a great deal of bitterness

00:56:00.190 --> 00:56:01.779
and frustration.

00:56:01.780 --> 00:56:03.399
And so to return to what I said

00:56:03.400 --> 00:56:05.469
earlier, in conclusion, we

00:56:05.470 --> 00:56:07.509
are not only here to talk about

00:56:07.510 --> 00:56:09.459
an important woman, but

00:56:09.460 --> 00:56:11.400
we're also here to right a wrong.

00:56:12.580 --> 00:56:14.259
And I would like to end this talk by

00:56:14.260 --> 00:56:16.389
quoting something of Peretz's that

00:56:16.390 --> 00:56:19.269
Oyerbakh repeated in 1945.

00:56:19.270 --> 00:56:21.819
When surveying the rubble of Warsaw,

00:56:21.820 --> 00:56:24.279
she thought back to the legacy

00:56:24.280 --> 00:56:26.319
of this great tribune of modern

00:56:26.320 --> 00:56:28.389
Yiddishkeit, who had once sought

00:56:28.390 --> 00:56:30.639
to inspire a new

00:56:30.640 --> 00:56:32.979
Jewish culture in Poland,

00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:35.109
in Eastern Europe, anchored

00:56:35.110 --> 00:56:37.029
in both Jewish tradition and European

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:38.139
humanism.

00:56:38.140 --> 00:56:40.809
And I use Anita Norich's translation

00:56:40.810 --> 00:56:42.736
from her book, Discovering Exile.

00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:45.609
"Console My People.

00:56:45.610 --> 00:56:47.949
It needs consolation, strengthen

00:56:47.950 --> 00:56:50.499
its heart, shore up its spirit.

00:56:50.500 --> 00:56:52.449
Don't be the wind that puts out the

00:56:52.450 --> 00:56:53.349
flame.

00:56:53.350 --> 00:56:55.689
Instead, pour oil and tend

00:56:55.690 --> 00:56:58.089
the wick because night is poison

00:56:58.090 --> 00:56:59.409
and despair.

00:56:59.410 --> 00:57:01.359
Death, console my people.

00:57:01.360 --> 00:57:02.979
It needs consolation."

00:57:04.300 --> 00:57:05.300
Thank you.

